# DepthWizard — Project Rules

## Context
Building a pipeline that turns a single optical satellite RGB image into a
metric Digital Surface Model (DSM), plus an interactive 3D flythrough viewer.
This is for Smart India Hackathon. Scoring is 50% DSM accuracy (RMSE/MAE/
correlation, stratified across urban/sparse/hilly/forested terrain) and
50% visualization quality and standalone deployment.

## Stack
Backend: Python 3.11, PyTorch, rasterio, FastAPI, OpenCV, scipy
Frontend: React 18 + TypeScript + Vite + Three.js (@react-three/fiber)
Packaging: Tauri + PyInstaller

## Non-negotiable rules
1. NEVER silently drop or reproject geospatial metadata. CRS and affine
   transform from input GeoTIFF must be preserved exactly in output.
2. NEVER let nodata pixels become 0m. Mask them explicitly and propagate
   the mask through every stage.
3. All height values in metres. Document vertical datum (EGM96 geoid vs
   WGS84 ellipsoid) in every function that touches elevation.
4. Every model output ships with a per-pixel uncertainty estimate.
5. No hardcoded paths. Config via pydantic Settings + .env.
6. Any function doing array math over a full raster gets a docstring stating
   input shape, dtype, units, and nodata convention.
7. Type hints everywhere. numpy arrays annotated with shape in the docstring.
8. No network calls at inference time in the packaged app — it must run
   fully offline. All external data is fetched at build/prep time and cached.

## Style
- Small, testable functions. Prefer pure functions over classes with state.
- Fail loudly with informative errors. Never return silently-wrong geometry.
- Comments explain WHY, not WHAT.
- Tests in pytest, colocated in tests/ mirroring the source tree.

## Things that are easy to get wrong here — be careful
- Depth models output INVERSE depth (disparity). Higher value = closer.
  For satellite nadir imagery, closer = taller. Get the sign right and
  assert it in tests.
- Depth Anything runs at ~518px. Satellite images are much larger.
  Tiled inference requires global scale alignment or you get seams.
- SRTM/Copernicus are DSMs from radar; the phase centre sits inside forest
  canopy, not on the ground and not at the treetop. Do not assume a simple
  bias direction — document which product and which reference surface.
- Shadow length -> height requires GSD. Georeferenced inputs only.
